<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canterbury</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Durovernum Cantiacorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canterbury is a cathedral city in Kent, south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. As Durovernum Cantiacorum it was the chief town of the Cantiaci under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of Kent, where Augustine founded in 597 the see whose archbishop heads the English Church. The murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-becket">Thomas Becket</a> in its cathedral in 1170 made it a great place of pilgrimage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canterbury.jpg" alt="Canterbury" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antony McCallum: Who is the uploader, photographer, full copyright owner and proprietor of WyrdLight.com · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canterbury-cathedral-wyrdlight.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church, under the archbishops of <b>Canterbury</b> and York, owned a large share of the land until Henry VIII dissolved the monasteries between 1536 and 1541.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…quality, including the carvings at Breedon on the Hill and the church at Brixworth, and Mercian scholars mattered to the later English revival: Alfred the Great recruited four Mercian clerics, among them Wærferth of Worcester and Plegmund, later archbishop of <b>Canterbury</b>, to help with his programme of learning.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Learning and royal service could carry a man of modest birth to these offices: Thomas Becket, a London merchant’s son, became Archbishop of <b>Canterbury</b> in 1162.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>It came after Henry's agents destroyed the shrine of Thomas Becket at <b>Canterbury</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Becket was canonised in 1173, and <b>Canterbury</b> became one of Europe's great pilgrimage sites.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-29">29 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under threat of prosecution, the Convocation of <b>Canterbury</b> acknowledged Henry as Supreme Head of the Church in England 'as far as the law of Christ allows'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-11">11 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
